      "id": "1fprcvf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Constitutional Articles Related to Parliament"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Parliament of India consists of three organs: the President, Rajya Sabha (Council of States), and Lok Sabha (House of the People).",
        "Rajya Sabha is a permanent continuous body not subject to dissolution, with one-third of its members retiring every second year.",
        "Lok Sabha has a normal tenure of 5 years from the date of its first meeting, but it can be dissolved earlier by the President.",
        "Money Bills (Article 110) can only be introduced in Lok Sabha on the prior recommendation of the President.",
        "A Joint Sitting (Article 108) is summoned by the President and presided over by the Speaker of Lok Sabha to resolve deadlocks on Ordinary Bills."
      ],
      "id": "4k45tad"
    },
